\subsubsection*{A\_4 in the Chain: The Convergence at *Praxis*} The chain of soul-functions that Aristotle elaborates across *De Anima* and *De Motu Animalium* does not terminate in a disembodied act of cognition; it converges, rather, at *praxis*---at the concrete deed performed by a living being whose entire somatic and psychic constitution has been mobilised toward a single end. We must therefore ask what structural position the convergence at *praxis* occupies within the broader analytic of motion (*kinēsis*) that governs Aristotle's psychology. Aristotle insists that the soul is not simply the locus of thought or perception considered in isolation; indeed, thinking itself "could not be without standing in the context of the entire life of a human being" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 149)[2]. Heidegger's paraphrase of the Aristotelian text is decisive here, for it makes visible what is perhaps the most consequential claim of the action-chain: that even the highest cognitive operation, *noein*, cannot extricate itself from *phantasia*, and therefore cannot extricate itself from the embodied totality of involvements in which the living being already finds itself. The convergence at *praxis* is thus not an addendum to the psychology of perception and desire; it is the structural telos toward which every preceding link---*aisthēsis*, *phantasia*, *orexis*, *nous praktikos*---is always already oriented. To appreciate the force of this convergence, we peculiar way in which Aristotle's account resists the separation of knowing from doing. The so-called "practical syllogism" has often been treated as though it were merely a logical schema applied to conduct, but its deeper significance lies in the way it binds the cognitive and the appetitive into a single kinetic unity. (Aristotle 2014, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) the calculative animal must possess "a single standard to measure by, for that is pursued which is greater" (*De Anima*, 434a7--10). The user is thus confronted not with a serial chain---first perception, then imagination, then desire, then action---but with a convergent structure in which each moment implicates the others simultaneously. Sensitive imagination, Aristotle notes, "is found in all animals, deliberative imagination only in those that are calculative" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 434a5--7)[3]. The distinction between these two grades of *phantasia* is not merely taxonomic; it signals that the convergence at *praxis* takes a qualitatively different form in beings capable of deliberation than it does in beings that act from immediate appetitive impulse alone. In the calculative animal, the convergence passes through a moment of comparative judgement---a weighing of presented goods---that is absent from the simpler functional loop of stimulus and response. Papachristou, accordingly, prefers to leave the Greek word *phantasia* untranslated, since its range of meaning far exceeds the English "imagination" (Papachristou, *Three Kinds or Grades of Phantasia in Aristotle's De Anima*, pp. 9--10)[4]. Heidegger's retrieval of the Aristotelian action-chain deepens this analysis by insisting that the convergence at *praxis* is not a terminal event added to a sequence but is, rather, the mode in which being-in-the-world is concretely enacted. On his reading, the *pathē*---the affective conditions through which the living being is always already disposed---define "being-in-the-world in a fundamental sense" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 129)[5]. Hence the convergence at *praxis* is inseparable from the affective tuning that precedes and accompanies every deliberate act. The deed is not produced by a chain of discrete causes operating in linear succession; it emerges from the totality of involvements---perceptual, imaginative, appetitive, affective---that constitutes the being of the acting creature at a given moment. The four-fold analytic (A\_4) is thus a structural schema, not a temporal narrative: *aisthēsis*, *phantasia*, *orexis*, and *hexis* converge at *praxis* in the way that the four *aitiai* converge upon the thing they explain, each illuminating a different but co-present aspect of a single kinetic event. Accordingly, the subsections that follow will trace the internal articulation of this convergence, beginning with the role of *phantasia* at the heart of every action. --- \subsubsection*{*Phantasia* at the Heart of Every Action} If *praxis* is the convergent telos of the soul's kinetic chain, then *phantasia* is perhaps the most structurally indispensable moment within that convergence, for it is the capacity by which the object of desire is made present---rendered *apparent*---to the acting being. Aristotle is explicit: the soul never thinks without a *phantasma*; and the practical soul, in particular, "calculates and deliberates what is to come by reference to what is present" by means of "the images or thoughts which are within the soul, just as if it were seeing" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 431b6--8)[6]. The virtual world that *phantasia* opens is not a secondary copy of reality; it is the medium in which the practical agent encounters its own situation *as* a situation calling for decision and response. In the *Rhetoric*, similarly, the force of *phantasia* is not confined to mere embellishment; Gonzalez argues that Aristotle "never entirely sidelines hypokrisis" and that *phantasia*, far from being "mere show," operates at the structural heart of rhetorical demonstration (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, p. 4)[7]. The continuity between the psychological and rhetorical registers is thus essential to grasping the scope of *phantasia*: in both, *phantasia* mediates between the given and the projected, between what is perceived and what is presented-as-worth-pursuing. We must distinguish, however, between at least two grades of *phantasia* that Aristotle himself delineates. Sensitive (*aisthētikē*) phantasia belongs to all animals and consists in the residual image left by perception; deliberative (*bouleutikē* or *logistikē*) phantasia belongs only to calculative beings and involves the comparative presentation of multiple possible goods. (Aristotle 2014, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) "whether this or that shall be enacted is already a task requiring calculation" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 434a7--8)[8]. The practical import of this distinction is considerable, for it means that in the calculative animal the *phantasma* is not merely a passive trace of a prior perception but an active construction---a projected image of a future state of affairs that the agent evaluates in light of a standard. The deliberative *phantasma* thus occupies a structural position between desire (*orexis*) and belief (*doxa*): it presents the object under a description that renders it desirable or aversive, and it does so with a kind of quasi-perceptual vividness that makes the future seem present. Aristotle puts this with characteristic precision: "perceiving by sense that the beacon is fire, it recognizes in virtue of the general faculty of sense that it signifies an enemy, because it sees it moving; but sometimes by means of the images or thoughts which are within the soul, just as if it were seeing, it calculates and deliberates what is to come by reference to what is present" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 431b3--8)[9]. The phrase "just as if it were seeing" (*hōsper horōn*) captures the essential character of deliberative *phantasia*: it is a seeing-without-present-object, a making-visible of what is not yet actual. This quasi-perceptual character raises the question of *phantasia*'s relation to *doxa* (belief or opinion). If *phantasia* can present an object as pleasant or painful independently of any settled judgement about its real worth, then the door is opened to cases in which *phantasia* and *doxa* diverge---cases in which one knows that the approaching object is harmless but nonetheless imagines it as fearful. Aristotle appears to acknowledge this possibility, and a full treatment of the tension between phantastic presentation and doxastic evaluation will occupy the subsection on the *content-conditional doxa-gate* below. Here it suffices *phantasia*'s voluntariness---its susceptibility to deliberate deployment, as when the rhetor places an image "before the eyes" of the audience---is a feature of the deliberative grade alone. Sensitive *phantasia*, by contrast, arises without voluntary control, as the automatic residue of perceptual contact with the world. Heidegger reads the centrality of *phantasia* in precisely these terms: *phantasia* is not a "faculty" set alongside other faculties but the "making-present-to-itself" of the world, without which no deliberation---and hence no *praxis*---would be possible (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 149)[10]. On this reading, *phantasia* is the hinge between the receptive moment of *aisthēsis* and the projective moment of *orexis*; it is the point at which the world is not merely received but *taken up* in a mode that opens possibilities for action. Similarly, von Uexküll's account of the perceptual world (*Umwelt*) of different animals illustrates how radically the image-world conditions the field of possible action: standing before a meadow, we are "forced to ask ourselves, 'Does the meadow present the same impression to the eyes of such different animals as it does to our eyes?'" (von Uexküll, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 169--171). The *phantasia* of each creature, we might say, constitutes the virtual world within which that creature's *praxis* unfolds. \subsubsection*{The Three-Factor Schema, the Three Entry Points} The convergence at *praxis* and the centrality of *phantasia* together yield a structural schema that we may articulate in terms of three co-present factors: *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos*. Each of these factors names an entry point through which the living being's disposition toward action can be inflected, redirected, or consolidated. The schema is not an invention imposed upon the Aristotelian text; it emerges from the text's own repeated insistence that the *pathē* (affective conditions), the *hexeis* (stable dispositions), and the *logoi* (articulate judgements) are the three determinants of character and, thereby, of action. Heidegger's reading of the *Rhetoric* makes this triadic structure visible in a distinctively ontological register: rhetoric, he notes, treats speaking "in such a way that it aims in itself at constructing a view: a *doxa* is to be cultivated" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 129)[11]. The cultivation of *doxa*, however, is never a purely cognitive affair; it passes through the affective dispositions of the audience and is stabilised (or destabilised) by their *hexeis*. Burke's account of rhetoric as the art of "identification" provides a complementary entry point. (Burke 1950, *A Rhetoric of Motives*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) the appetite and will are altered by "religion, opinion, and apprehension"---three domains that map, with illuminating precision, onto the three factors of the Aristotelian schema (Burke, *A Rhetoric of Motives*, pp. 93--96)[12]. Religion corresponds to the deep *hexis* that orients the will prior to any particular deliberation; opinion corresponds to the doxastic moment that filters the content of *phantasia*; apprehension corresponds to the affective colouring---the *pathos*---that attends every encounter with a presented good. Burke's triad is not identical with Aristotle's, but the structural homology is instructive: both thinkers recognise that persuasion---and, by extension, action---is never the result of a single cause operating in isolation but emerges from the interplay of affective, dispositional, and cognitive factors. Von Uexküll's functional cycle (*Funktionskreis*) offers a third entry point, this time from the side of biology. In the functional cycle, the organism's receptor organs (*Merkorgan*) and effector organs (*Wirkorgan*) are coupled through a perceptual sign (*Merkmal*) and an operational sign (*Wirkmal*) that together constitute the organism's *Umwelt* (von Uexküll, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 122--126). The three-factor schema of *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos* can be read as an enrichment of the functional cycle for the case of the *logon echon*---the speaking, deliberating animal. Where the simple organism's *Umwelt* is constituted by fixed perceptual and operational signs, the human agent's world is permeated by affective attunement (*pathos*), sedimented into stable dispositions (*hexis*), and articulated through judgement (*logos*). The three entry points are thus not merely analytic distinctions; they name the three dimensions along which the totality of involvements can be accessed---and, crucially, along which rhetorical intervention can operate. \subsubsection*{Three Types of Action} The structural schema developed above allows us to distinguish three types of action, differentiated by the route through which *phantasia* activates *orexis* and issues in bodily movement. These three types correspond to three configurations of the convergent chain, each defined by the relative prominence of *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos* within the total kinetic event. They are not exclusive categories but ideal types, each admitting of degrees and mixed cases; indeed, most concrete actions will exhibit features of more than one type. Nevertheless, the typology is analytically indispensable, for it reveals how the same basic psycho-somatic architecture---the same chain from *aisthēsis* through *phantasia* and *orexis* to *praxis*---can generate qualitatively different modes of conduct. **Type I: *Pathē*-Driven Action.** In this type, the dominant factor is the affective condition (*pathos*) that grips the agent prior to any deliberation. The *phantasma* that presents the object of desire or aversion is charged with affective intensity; it is, as it were, saturated with the colour of the prevailing mood. Heidegger captures this with his characteristic insistence on the bodily and situational character of the *pathē*: \begin{quote}
Being-angry is something like a being-in-motion of the body constituted thus and so, of a corporeality that finds itself in a fully determinate mode, or a body part, and thus it is a fully determinate motion under pressure from this and that, from definite circumstances because of this and that occasion. (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 150--152)[13]
\end{quote} The phrase "under pressure from this and that" signals the characteristic feature of *pathē*-driven action: the agent is moved *by* the situation rather than moving *through* it by deliberate choice. The *phantasma* need not pass through the doxa-gate; it activates *orexis* directly, producing what Aristotle calls involuntary movements "without the intellect or desire having previously deliberated." The *resonant kinēsis* in this case is immediate: the affective charge of the perceived situation reverberates through the somatic constitution and issues in action before any comparative weighing of goods can occur. The anger that moves the hand to strike, the fear that moves the legs to flee---these are paradigmatic instances of Type I action, in which the *pathetic* loop operates at its shortest and most intense. **Type II: *Hexis*-Formed Action.** Here the dominant factor is the stable disposition (*hexis*) that the agent has acquired through repeated practice. Heidegger emphasises that *hexis* is not merely habitual automatism but a mode of "being-composed" (*Sich-befinden*) that determines how the agent encounters and responds to situations (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 147)[14]. The *phantasma* in *hexis*-formed action is shaped by the accumulated history of the agent's prior engagements; it presents the object not under the vivid colouring of immediate affect but under the steady light of trained perception. The virtuous person, for instance, does not deliberate anew in each situation about whether courage is called for; the *hexis* of courage has already configured the perceptual field so that the dangerous situation is apprehended as an occasion for steadfastness rather than flight. The *resonant aisthēma*---the perceptual trace that feeds back into the dispositional structure---is here a slow, cumulative process of diachronic sedimentation rather than a sudden synchronic discharge. Aristotle's account of *hexis* in the *Metaphysics* underscores that it designates a stable condition (*diathesis*) of the soul that is difficult to dislodge, and Heidegger reads this stability as the ontological basis for character: \begin{quote}
On the basis of a more precise understanding of what is meant by *hexis*, we will understand the analysis of the *pathē*, seeing how what is designated as *pathos* defines being-in-the-world in a fundamental sense, and how it comes into consideration as such a basic determination of being-in-the-world with the cultivation of *krisis*, of "taking-a-position," of "deciding" a critical question. (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 129)[15]
\end{quote} The passage makes clear that *hexis* and *pathos* are not independent but structurally intertwined: it is the *hexis* that determines how a given *pathos* is taken up and channelled into action. **Type III: *Logos*-Guided Action.** In this type, the dominant factor is the articulate judgement (*logos*) through which the agent evaluates the *phantasma* in light of general principles and specific circumstances. (Aristotle 2014, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) the deliberative animal must possess "a single standard to measure by" (Aristotle 2014, *On The Soul (De Anima)[1]*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]), and it is the function of *logos* to supply that standard. The *phantasma* here is not merely received (as in Type I) or pre-formed by habit (as in Type II); it is subjected to a comparative assessment that weighs the presented good against alternative possibilities. The *content-conditional doxa-gate* is fully operative in this type: the agent's settled beliefs (*doxai*) filter the content of the *phantasma*, accepting or rejecting its practical import on the basis of reasoned evaluation. Rhetoric, as Aristotle conceives it, operates primarily at this level: the rhetor's task is to present *phantasmata* that will pass through the audience's doxa-gate and activate the appropriate *orexis*. Gonzalez, in his analysis of *phantasia* in *Rhetoric* III.1, argues that rhetorical demonstration "can be compelling to one, yet fail to convince another," precisely because the doxa-gate is calibrated differently in different listeners (Gonzalez, *The Meaning and Function of Phantasia in Aristotle's 'Rhetoric' III.1*, pp. 14--15)[16]. The *resonant epithymia*---the desire that issues from the convergence of *logos* and *phantasia*---is thus a desire shaped by judgement, and the action to which it gives rise is the most fully articulated form of *praxis*. These three types are not sealed compartments but moments of a single kinetic continuum. Most human actions involve elements of all three; what varies is the relative weight and the structural route through the convergent chain. The typology is intended not to classify actions exhaustively but to make visible the different configurations of *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos* that the Aristotelian architecture can generate. This term designates the point in the chain at which the *phantasma*---the image presented by *phantasia* to *orexis*---is subjected to evaluation by the agent's settled beliefs (*doxai*). The gate is "content-conditional" because it does not operate uniformly on every *phantasma*; rather, its activation depends upon the specific content of the image and the specific configuration of beliefs that the agent brings to the encounter. Some *phantasmata* pass through the gate without resistance, because they are consonant with the agent's existing *doxai*; others are blocked or modified; still others bypass the gate altogether, activating *orexis* directly through the affective channel of Type I action. Aristotle's distinction between sensitive and deliberative *phantasia* provides the structural basis for this differential gating. Sensitive *phantasia*, we have noted, arises automatically from perception and does not require the agent's voluntary participation; it operates, as it were, below the threshold of doxastic evaluation. Deliberative *phantasia*, by contrast, involves the active construction of projected goods and their comparative assessment---a process that necessarily engages the agent's beliefs about what is truly good, expedient, or noble. (Aristotle 2014, *On The Soul (De Anima)*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) "when it makes a pronouncement, as in the case of sensation it pronounces the object to be pleasant or painful" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 431b8--10)[17]. The pronouncement of pleasure or pain is the most basic output of the doxa-gate: it attaches a practical valence to the *phantasma*, rendering it an object of pursuit or avoidance. The rhetorical significance of the doxa-gate is considerable. In the *Rhetoric*, Aristotle insists that "maxims always produce this effect, because the utterance of them amounts to a general declaration of what should be chosen; so that, if the maxims are sound, they display the speaker as a man of sound character" (Aristotle, *Rhetoric*, 1395b13--17)[18]. Maxims operate, we may say, by recalibrating the audience's doxa-gate: they supply or reinforce the general beliefs against which particular *phantasmata* will be evaluated. A well-placed maxim can lower the threshold for acceptance of a particular image of the good, while an ill-chosen maxim can raise it. The rhetor's art, accordingly, consists not merely in presenting vivid images but in managing the doxastic conditions under which those images will be received. Heidegger's reading clarifies this by emphasising that *doxa* is not a private mental state but a mode of public being-in-the-world. The "taking-a-position" (*krisis*) that activates the doxa-gate is always already shaped by the communal *hexeis* and shared articulations within which the agent lives (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 129)[19]. Hence the doxa-gate is never purely individual; it is socially constituted and rhetorically accessible. The user is therefore positioned within a web of public meanings that condition, in advance, which *phantasmata* will be accepted as grounds for action and which will be dismissed. The *content-conditional* character of the gate reflects the fact that different contents engage different strata of belief---ethical, political, aesthetic, prudential---and that the same agent may exhibit a stringent gate for one domain and a permeable gate for another. \subsubsection*{*Hexis* Bivalence: *Technē*-Hexis and *Praxis*-Hexis} Aristotle's concept of *hexis* is not univocal; it admits of a fundamental bivalence that is decisive for the present analysis. On the one hand, *hexis* names the stable disposition acquired through the practice of a *technē*---the skill of the builder, the musician, the rhetorician. On the other hand, *hexis* names the stable disposition that constitutes ethical character---the virtue or vice that determines how the agent will respond to situations calling for courage, temperance, or justice. We may designate these, respectively, as *technē*-hexis and *praxis*-hexis, while noting that the distinction is structural rather than absolute: every *technē*-hexis involves a moment of *praxis* (the builder must *decide* to build), and every *praxis*-hexis involves a moment of *technē* (the courageous person must *know how* to stand firm). Heidegger's treatment of *hexis* in *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* illuminates this bivalence by insisting that *hexis* is not a static possession but a dynamic mode of being-disposed. He glosses *hexis* as "being-composed" and emphasises that it involves a "being-able-to-see, to see the subject matter in person" that is activated when the knower moves from *hexis* (latent knowing) to *energeia* (actual knowing) (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 147)[20]. The transition from *hexis* to *energeia* is itself a *kinēsis*---a movement---but one that "cannot be properly designated" by the usual categories of change, because it is not the acquisition of a new quality but the activation of an already-possessed capacity. This peculiar character of the *hexis*-to-*energeia* transition is what distinguishes both *technē*-hexis and *praxis*-hexis from the merely affective movements of the *pathē*: whereas a *pathos* simply befalls the agent, the activation of a *hexis* involves the agent's own readiness and capacity. The bivalence becomes visible when we consider the different roles that the doxa-gate plays in each type. In *technē*-hexis, the doxa-gate is calibrated to the standards of the craft: the builder evaluates the *phantasma* of the projected house against the principles of sound construction, and the gate admits only those images that conform to the relevant *technē*. The *hexis* of craft, accordingly, is a disposition toward correctness---toward the production of an object that meets an external standard. In *praxis*-hexis, by contrast, the doxa-gate is calibrated to the standards of the good life: the virtuous agent evaluates the *phantasma* of the presented situation against a conception of human flourishing, and the gate admits only those images that conduce to virtuous action. The *hexis* of virtue, accordingly, is a disposition toward the mean---toward the right action, at the right time, in the right way. Rickert's account of disclosure as "itself manifold and ambient" provides a further lens through which to view this bivalence (Rickert, *Ambient Rhetoric*, pp. 88--92)[21]. If disclosure can be "performed, practiced, and activated in many ways, only so much of which will at any given time be directly appropriable to consciousness, intention, or theorization," then *hexis* names precisely the sedimented dimension of disclosure that operates below the threshold of explicit awareness. The *technē*-hexis discloses the world as a field of makeable things; the *praxis*-hexis discloses the world as a field of doable things. Both are modes of *articulational concretion*---that is, both represent the concretisation of general capacities into specific, situationally responsive dispositions. The bivalence of *hexis* is thus not a defect in Aristotle's conceptual apparatus but a structural reflection of the double character of human agency: we are beings who both make and do, who both produce external objects and realise internal excellences. The significance of this bivalence for von Uexküll's biology should not be overlooked. Von Uexküll's organism, immersed in its *Umwelt*, possesses what we might call a biological analogue of *hexis*: the innate operational schema that determines how the organism will respond to its perceptual signs. Yet the organism's *hexis* is, in von Uexküll's terms, fixed by the species-specific plan (*Bauplan*), whereas the human agent's *hexis* is acquired, malleable, and subject to rhetorical and ethical cultivation (von Uexküll, *A Foray Into the Worlds of Animals and Humans with A Theory of Meaning*, pp. 169--171). The gap between biological fixity and ethical plasticity is precisely the space within which rhetoric operates: the rhetor addresses an audience whose *hexeis* are not given by nature but formed by culture, education, and deliberate practice. \subsubsection*{The *Pathetic* Loop: Synchronic Pass and Diachronic Sedimentation} The *pathetic* loop names the recursive structure by which *pathē* are not merely experienced as transient episodes but feed back into the dispositional constitution of the agent, thereby reshaping the very conditions under which future *pathē* will arise. The loop operates on two timescales: the synchronic pass, in which a *pathos* grips the agent in the present moment and issues in immediate action or inhibition; and the diachronic sedimentation, through which repeated synchronic passes gradually consolidate into stable *hexeis*. The dual-resonance of the loop---its simultaneous operation on both timescales---is what makes the *pathē* constitutive of character rather than merely incidental to it. Heidegger's analysis of *pathos* in *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* distinguishes four general meanings of the term, of which the most important for the present analysis are the "variable condition" and the specifically ontological meaning connected with *paschein*, "what one most often translates as 'suffering'" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 127)[22]. The ontological meaning is decisive because it locates *pathos* not in the subjective interior of a mental substance but in the being-in-the-world of the living creature: "this being-taken of being-there as being-in-its-world does not involve anything like what we could designate as the 'spiritual,' which invites the conception of *pathos* as affect" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 148)[23]. The *pathetic* loop, accordingly, is not a psychological mechanism operating within a self-enclosed mind; it is a structural feature of the way in which Dasein's being-in-the-world is affectively constituted and reconstituted over time. The synchronic pass of the *pathetic* loop can be described as follows. A perceptual encounter (*aisthēsis*) generates a *phantasma* charged with affective valence; this *phantasma* activates *orexis* and, depending on whether the doxa-gate is engaged, issues in action of Type I, Type II, or Type III. But the action itself produces a new perceptual encounter---the agent sees the effect of what it has done, feels the resistance or compliance of the world---and this new encounter generates a new *phantasma*, which feeds back into the loop. The *resonant kinēsis* is thus not a single arc from perception to action but a self-amplifying or self-dampening cycle whose dynamics depend on the match or mismatch between the projected good and the experienced outcome. In the case of anger, for instance, the synchronic pass may escalate: the initial *phantasma* of insult generates angry action, which provokes retaliation, which generates a new *phantasma* of insult, and so on. Heidegger captures this escalatory potential in his description of being-angry as "a fully determinate motion under pressure from this and that, from definite circumstances because of this and that occasion" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, pp. 150--152)[24]. The diachronic sedimentation of the *pathetic* loop is the process by which repeated synchronic passes consolidate into *hexeis*. (Heidegger 2009, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. [PAGE NEEDED]) *hexis* stands "in relation to the *pathē*" as "our clue to the more precise conception of the being-structure of the *pathē* themselves" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 145)[25]. This means that the *pathē* are not merely raw affects that the *hexis* subsequently organises; rather, the *hexis* is itself the sedimented history of the *pathē*---the cumulative trace of innumerable synchronic passes through the *pathetic* loop. The irascible person is not someone who happens to get angry frequently; rather, the repeated experience of anger has formed a *hexis* that disposes the agent to perceive situations as provocative and to respond with anger. The *pathetic* loop is thus the mechanism through which character is formed, and the point at which Aristotle's psychology intersects most directly with his ethics. \subsubsection*{Synthesis: Emotion as the Affective Architecture of Being-in-the-World} The analysis pursued across the preceding subsections yields a unified picture of emotion that is irreducible to any single one of its constituent moments. Emotion, on the Aristotelian-Heideggerian account we have been developing, is not a discrete internal state appended to cognition from without; it is the affective architecture of being-in-the-world---the structural configuration of *pathē*, *hexeis*, and *logoi* through which the living being encounters its situation as mattering, as calling for response, as charged with practical significance. The convergence at *praxis* (A\_4) reveals that every action emerges from the simultaneous co-presence of perceptual, imaginative, appetitive, and affective factors; *phantasia* at the heart of every action discloses the medium in which the object of desire is made present; the three-factor schema identifies the entry points through which the affective architecture can be inflected; the three types of action map the structural routes by which different configurations of *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos* issue in conduct; the *content-conditional doxa-gate* specifies the mechanism by which belief filters the practical import of *phantasmata*; the bivalence of *hexis* distinguishes the productive from the ethical dimension of stable disposition; and the *pathetic* loop traces the recursive dynamics by which transient affects sediment into enduring character. The mandatory theoretical synthesis between Aristotle's *aisthēsis* and Heidegger's *Befindlichkeit* provides the keystone of this architecture. The critical parallel lies in the *receptive* character of both concepts: just as *aisthēsis* is a mode of being-affected by the world that precedes and conditions all cognitive elaboration, so *Befindlichkeit* is the "being-taken" (*Genommenwerden*) of Dasein by its situation that discloses the world prior to any deliberate act of knowing or willing. Aristotle holds that "perceiving, and also both thinking and knowing, are, on their own assumption, ways of being affected or moved" (Aristotle, *De Anima*, 405b11--12)[26]. Heidegger, reading *aisthēsis* through the lens of *Befindlichkeit*, insists that this receptivity is not a deficiency to be overcome by the sovereign intellect but the very condition of possibility for any encounter with the world at all (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 148)[27]. The bridge between *aisthēsis* and *Befindlichkeit* is thus not a loose analogy but a structural identity at the deepest level of the analytic: both name the primordial receptivity through which the world is disclosed as affectively significant. Accordingly, emotion as the affective architecture of being-in-the-world is not a subjective colouring superimposed upon a neutral perceptual field; it is the field itself, insofar as the field is always already disclosed through mood, disposition, and practical concern. The user is always already within this architecture; there is no standpoint outside affective involvement from which the world could be encountered as a mere collection of value-free objects. The totality of involvements that Heidegger names "being-in-the-world" is, from the Aristotelian side, the totality of *pathē*, *hexeis*, and *logoi* through which the psyche's kinetic chain converges at *praxis*. The synthesis confirms that emotion is not an epiphenomenon of action but its enabling condition---the affective medium through which every perception, every imagination, and every desire acquires its practical orientation. \bigskip \footnote{The systematic connection between the Aristotelian *pathē* and Heidegger's analysis of *Stimmung* (attunement) and *Befindlichkeit* (disposedness) in *Being and Time* will be developed at length in the chapter on rhetoric and attunement. There, the alignment between *aisthēsis* and *Befindlichkeit*---their shared receptive character---will be extended attunement conditions the possibility of rhetorical address: the speaker does not merely produce affects in the audience but discloses a shared world in which certain affective configurations become possible. Similarly, the *pathetic* loop will be reinterpreted in terms of the temporal structure of *Dasein*'s care (*Sorge*), and the bivalence of *hexis* will be related to Heidegger's distinction between authentic and inauthentic modes of being-toward-death. At each point, Heidegger's retrieval of Aristotelian concepts has been invoked to deepen and redirect the analysis; however, the full development of the Heideggerian dimension---particularly the existential analytic of *Being and Time* and the lecture courses on Aristotle---remains a task for subsequent sections of the dissertation. Several promissory notes are in order. First, with respect to the convergence at *praxis* (A\_4), Heidegger's reading of *kinēsis* as a mode of being-determined in its "there" will need to be developed in connection with *Dasein*'s care-structure (*Sorge*), which is the existential analogue of the Aristotelian practical chain (BT \S41, H.191; Eng.~p.~236). Second, with respect to *phantasia*, Heidegger's gloss of *phantasia* as "making-present-to-itself" must be related to the ecstatic temporality of *Dasein*---specifically, to the moment of *Gegenwärtigen* (making-present) that constitutes the present dimension of care (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 149)[28]. Third, the three-factor schema requires further articulation in light of Heidegger's tripartite structure of disclosedness: *Befindlichkeit*, understanding (*Verstehen*), and discourse (*Rede*), which map provocatively---though not straightforwardly---onto *pathos*, *hexis*, and *logos*. Fourth, the three types of action need to be re-examined against Heidegger's distinction between circumspective concern (*Besorgen*) and solicitude (*Fürsorge*) (BT \S26, H.121; Eng.~p.~159). Fifth, the *content-conditional doxa-gate* invites comparison with Heidegger's analysis of public interpretedness (*Ausgelegtheit*) and idle talk (*Gerede*), in which communal *doxai* function as the pre-given horizon against which individual judgement operates. Sixth, the bivalence of *hexis* calls for development in terms of Heidegger's analysis of authenticity and inauthenticity, which represent, perhaps, the existential transformation of the Aristotelian distinction between *technē*-hexis and *praxis*-hexis. Seventh, the *pathetic* loop---particularly its diachronic sedimentation---must be related to Heidegger's account of historicality (*Geschichtlichkeit*) and the way in which *Dasein*'s past is not merely elapsed but actively taken up and reiterated in present comportment. Eighth, the synthesis of emotion as affective architecture connects directly to *Stimmung* discloses "Being-in-the-world as a whole" and thus constitutes the ontological ground for any regional analysis of specific emotions (BT \S29, H.137; Eng.~p.~176). The development of these connections will draw upon both *Being and Time* and the *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy* lectures, and will be undertaken in the chapters on attunement and rhetoric that follow. ```
